# Copy to .env.selfhost or run scripts/selfhost/install.sh to generate one.
# The installer writes real secrets and refuses to overwrite an existing file.
#
# NOTE: `docker compose up` does NOT auto-load .env.selfhost (Compose only
# auto-loads a file literally named .env). Bring the stack up with the
# installer, or pass the file explicitly — with the mode overlays matching the
# SELFHOST_*_MODE flags below (see deploy/selfhost/), and the same
# --project-name the installer/scripts use, or the stack runs under a
# different project (separate volumes) than backup/restore/upgrade expect.
# For the default bundled stack:
#   docker compose --project-name codemagic-patch-selfhost --env-file .env.selfhost \
#     -f docker-compose.selfhost.yml -f deploy/selfhost/compose.bundled-db.yml \
#     -f deploy/selfhost/compose.bundled-storage.yml up -d
# A plain-http deployment (SELFHOST_SCHEME=http below) adds
#   -f deploy/selfhost/compose.http.yml
# after the storage overlay.

# Over https (the default) a fully-qualified domain a certificate can be
# issued for. Over plain http (SELFHOST_SCHEME=http) it is localhost, and
# only that: plain http serves the machine the stack runs on.
CODEMAGIC_PATCH_API_DOMAIN=updates.example.com
# Bundled storage only — drop this line in the s3/gcs storage modes, which
# have no storage domain (see STACK SHAPE below). Must differ from the API
# domain (two Caddy sites). Over plain http it is localhost:<port>
# (localhost:9110; not 80 or 443), with SELFHOST_HTTP_STORAGE_PORT below set
# to the same port.
CODEMAGIC_PATCH_STORAGE_DOMAIN=storage-updates.example.com
# Also required over plain http, where no certificate is issued: Caddy's
# `email` option refuses an empty value and would fail the whole config.
ACME_EMAIL=admin@example.com

# Both follow SELFHOST_SCHEME: http://... on a plain-http deployment.
SERVER_URL=https://updates.example.com
# With bundled storage this is the storage domain plus the /codemagic-patch
# prefix, as below. In the s3/gcs modes you set it yourself to the public https
# URL clients download artifacts from — the bucket, or the CDN in front of it
# (an http:// URL is accepted only on a plain-http deployment, and only on
# localhost).
PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://storage-updates.example.com/codemagic-patch

CODEMAGIC_PATCH_SERVER_IMAGE=codemagic-patch-server:selfhost
# Caddy image bundling the web dashboard; pin to a previous image to roll back.
CODEMAGIC_PATCH_CADDY_IMAGE=codemagic-patch-caddy:selfhost

# ── STACK SHAPE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The maintenance scripts (install/backup/restore/upgrade) assemble the compose
# stack from these flags. They are fixed at install time: switching a mode
# later means migrating data by hand — the scripts do not perform it.
# SELFHOST_DATABASE_MODE=bundled runs Postgres inside the stack using the
# POSTGRES_* values below; external connects the server to an existing
# PostgreSQL via DATABASE_URL, and the POSTGRES_* values are then unused and
# may be omitted. SELFHOST_STORAGE_MODE=bundled runs MinIO inside the stack
# behind CODEMAGIC_PATCH_STORAGE_DOMAIN using the MINIO_ROOT_* values below;
# s3 and gcs connect the server to an existing bucket instead. In the external
# storage modes CODEMAGIC_PATCH_STORAGE_DOMAIN and MINIO_ROOT_* are unused
# (external storage has no storage domain; they apply only to bundled
# storage), the storage overlay pins STORAGE_ADAPTER itself, and
# PUBLIC_BASE_URL is set by the operator to the public https URL clients
# download artifacts from — the bucket, or the CDN in front of it — instead of
# being derived from the storage domain.
SELFHOST_DATABASE_MODE=bundled
SELFHOST_STORAGE_MODE=bundled
# direct is the normal viewer-to-Caddy path. Bundled CloudFront changes this
# to cdn-origin and requires the separate origin domain/secrets below. External
# s3/gcs storage must stay direct because CloudFront targets the bucket itself.
SELFHOST_STORAGE_ORIGIN_MODE=direct
# https (the default, also what an absent line means): Caddy obtains Let's
# Encrypt certificates, which needs public DNS and ports 80/443. http: plain
# HTTP on port 80 for the machine the stack runs on alone (localhost), no
# certificates; written by `install.sh --allow-http` for a laptop evaluation
# of the real stack. It selects deploy/selfhost/compose.http.yml, every URL in
# this file follows it, and the OAuth callback the provider must allow becomes
# http://localhost/auth/callback. A server other machines reach needs https.
# Not combinable with a CDN adapter (DELIVERY_ADAPTER below stays base-url).
# Changing it on a live deployment is a reinstall: every URL clients were
# given changes with it.
SELFHOST_SCHEME=https
# Plain http + bundled storage only: the port the storage site listens on —
# the :port of CODEMAGIC_PATCH_STORAGE_DOMAIN (localhost:<port>). The
# plain-HTTP overlay publishes it on 127.0.0.1 (9110 when the key is absent);
# the installer keeps it in step with the domain, and a rerun refuses a
# mismatch.
# SELFHOST_HTTP_STORAGE_PORT=9110
# Required for SELFHOST_DATABASE_MODE=external:
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@db.example.com:5432/codemagic_patch
# For SELFHOST_STORAGE_MODE=s3 — S3_BUCKET is required; the rest are optional
# (omit S3_ENDPOINT for AWS, set it for S3-compatible providers; set the key
# pair together, or omit both to use the SDK's default credential chain, e.g.
# EC2/ECS instance roles):
# S3_BUCKET=my-ota-bucket
# S3_INTERNAL_BUCKET=my-private-bucket # optional; a distinct private bucket
# S3_REGION=us-east-1
# S3_ENDPOINT=
# S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=false
# S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# For SELFHOST_STORAGE_MODE=gcs — both buckets are required and must differ
# (the public bucket is world-readable, the internal one must never be). The
# server authenticates with the service-account key at
# <repo root>/gcs-service-account.json (gitignored), which the gcs overlay
# bind-mounts into the container:
# GCS_PUBLIC_BUCKET=my-ota-public
# GCS_INTERNAL_BUCKET=my-ota-internal

# ── SECRETS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every REPLACE_ME_* value below that your modes actually use MUST be replaced
# before you start the stack (the POSTGRES_* and MINIO_ROOT_* blocks belong to
# the bundled modes only — see the per-block markers below).
# These are placeholders, NOT usable defaults: this file is committed to the
# public repo, so a verbatim copy would run with publicly-known secrets.
# `scripts/selfhost/install.sh` generates strong random values for you — only set
# these by hand if you are not using the installer. As a guard against forgetting,
# the server REFUSES TO BOOT while WORKER_SHARED_SECRET or
# OAUTH_CLI_AUTH_SECRET are shorter than 32 chars (the placeholders are),
# so a verbatim copy fails fast instead of running with a known secret.
#
# Single-quote any value that contains a dollar sign (e.g.
# DATABASE_URL='postgresql://user:pa$word@host:5432/db'): docker compose
# expands $ sequences in unquoted values, silently mangling the value the
# container receives, while a single-quoted value stays literal. The installer
# writes connection URLs, tokens, and secrets single-quoted for this reason.
# SELFHOST_DATABASE_MODE=bundled only. With the external database these are
# unused — omit them and set DATABASE_URL instead.
POSTGRES_DB=codemagic_patch
POSTGRES_USER=codemagic_patch
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=REPLACE_ME_postgres_password

# SELFHOST_STORAGE_MODE=bundled only. There is no MinIO to credential in the
# s3/gcs modes — omit these two.
MINIO_ROOT_USER=codemagicpatchminio
MINIO_ROOT_PASSWORD=REPLACE_ME_minio_password

WORKER_SHARED_SECRET=REPLACE_ME_worker_secret

MODE=all
RUN_MIGRATIONS=true
LOGGER=true

# SELFHOST_STORAGE_MODE=bundled only — these three describe the stack's own
# MinIO (path-style addressing is a MinIO requirement, not an AWS one). DELETE
# them in the s3/gcs modes and use the S3_*/GCS_* block under STACK SHAPE
# above: the storage overlay pins STORAGE_ADAPTER itself, and because compose
# reads this file top to bottom with the LAST assignment winning, leaving
# S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=true here would override the false you set up there and
# send path-style requests to AWS.
STORAGE_ADAPTER=s3
S3_REGION=us-east-1
S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=true

# Cache-Control for the mutable delivery JSON (meta.json, manifest.json).
# Leave it unset: the server derives it from DELIVERY_ADAPTER below —
# "no-cache, must-revalidate" for base-url, where purge is a no-op and
# nothing could shorten a shared cache's TTL, and
# "public, max-age=0, s-maxage=300, must-revalidate" behind Cloudflare or
# CloudFront, where a failed purge is bounded by the five-minute window.
# MANIFEST_CACHE_CONTROL="public, max-age=0, s-maxage=300, must-revalidate"

# Delivery / CDN. Default (base-url): clients fetch artifacts straight from
# PUBLIC_BASE_URL. Set DELIVERY_ADAPTER=cloudflare to front the storage domain
# with Cloudflare (proxy that same domain) and have the server purge the edge
# cache after each release. When enabled, CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN (an API Token
# scoped to Zone > Cache Purge — NOT the legacy Global API Key) and
# CLOUDFLARE_ZONE_ID (the zone containing the storage domain) are REQUIRED;
# CLOUDFLARE_API_BASE_URL is an optional override.
# DELIVERY_ADAPTER=cloudflare
# CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=REPLACE_ME_cloudflare_cache_purge_token
# CLOUDFLARE_ZONE_ID=REPLACE_ME_cloudflare_zone_id
# CLOUDFLARE_API_BASE_URL=https://api.cloudflare.com/client/v4

# CloudFront alternative (do not enable together with Cloudflare). Required in
# every storage mode: the distribution id and either BOTH purge-key values or
# neither (the latter uses the AWS SDK default credential chain, e.g. an
# EC2/ECS role). Scope this identity to cloudfront:CreateInvalidation on one
# distribution; never put broad AWS credentials here.
# DELIVERY_ADAPTER=cloudfront
# CLOUDFRONT_DISTRIBUTION_ID=E123EXAMPLE
# CLOUDFRONT_ACCESS_KEY_ID=REPLACE_ME_cloudfront_purge_access_key
# CLOUDFRONT_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=REPLACE_ME_cloudfront_purge_secret_key
#
# Bundled storage + CloudFront only: set SELFHOST_STORAGE_ORIGIN_MODE above to
# cdn-origin and configure the distribution to send the fixed custom header
# X-Codemagic-Patch-Origin-Verify with the current secret. Must be non-empty.
# CODEMAGIC_PATCH_STORAGE_ORIGIN_DOMAIN=origin-storage-updates.example.com
# CLOUDFRONT_ORIGIN_VERIFY_SECRET=REPLACE_ME_cloudfront_origin_current
#
# _PREVIOUS is optional and only set while a rotation is in flight: current=new
# and previous=old until CloudFront reports Deployed, then delete the line
# again. Caddy accepts both slots, so a rotation never opens a 403 window.
# Leave it out otherwise — it falls back to the current secret.
# CLOUDFRONT_ORIGIN_VERIFY_SECRET_PREVIOUS=REPLACE_ME_cloudfront_origin_old

# GitHub OAuth — the default sign-in provider. The server refuses to boot
# unless at least one provider (GitHub here, or Bitbucket/GitLab below) is
# configured; the installer prompts for GitHub unless you configure
# Bitbucket or GitLab instead (then GitHub may be omitted entirely). The OAuth App
# needs an Authorization callback URL of https://<API domain>/auth/callback
# and a client secret; both the dashboard sign-in and the CLI's browser
# login (`cmpatch login`) ride on it.
# OAUTH_CLI_AUTH_SECRET is a local random secret (at least 32 chars) that
# signs the CLI browser-login authorization codes. Installs that predate the
# rename can keep their OAUTH_DEVICE_POLL_TOKEN_SECRET line — the server
# reads it as a permanent fallback.
GITHUB_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=REPLACE_ME_github_client_id
GITHUB_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=REPLACE_ME_github_client_secret
OAUTH_CLI_AUTH_SECRET=REPLACE_ME_oauth_cli_auth_secret
GITHUB_OAUTH_SCOPES="read:user user:email"

# Optional server-side hardening: allowlist for the web flow's redirect URI
# (comma-separated exact matches). Left unset (the default below), the check is
# skipped and web sign-in works. `scripts/selfhost/install.sh` sets this to your
# API domain for you. If you enable it by hand, the value MUST exactly match the
# callback the dashboard sends — https://<your API domain>/auth/callback — or web
# sign-in fails with an opaque `invalid_grant`. Replace updates.example.com first.
# GITHUB_OAUTH_ALLOWED_REDIRECT_URIS=https://updates.example.com/auth/callback

# Bitbucket Cloud sign-in — a second provider next to GitHub, or the sole
# provider when GitHub is omitted. Create an
# OAuth consumer (workspace settings → OAuth consumers) with callback URL
# https://<API domain>/auth/callback and the `account` + `email` scopes.
# Bitbucket scopes live ON THE CONSUMER (not in the authorize URL), and its
# callback matching is prefix-based, so one consumer serves exactly one
# dashboard origin. Both values are required together: the server refuses to
# boot with a client id but no secret (Bitbucket has no secret-less flow).
# BITBUCKET_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=REPLACE_ME_bitbucket_key
# BITBUCKET_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=REPLACE_ME_bitbucket_secret
# Optional hardening, same semantics as the GitHub allowlist above.
# BITBUCKET_OAUTH_ALLOWED_REDIRECT_URIS=https://updates.example.com/auth/callback

# GitLab sign-in — next to GitHub/Bitbucket, or a provider when they are
# omitted. Create an application (User Settings → Applications, or an admin
# area application) with callback URL https://<API domain>/auth/callback,
# the `read_user` scope, and Confidential checked. Both values are required
# together: the server refuses to boot with a client id but no secret.
# GITLAB_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=REPLACE_ME_gitlab_application_id
# GITLAB_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=REPLACE_ME_gitlab_secret
# GITLAB_OAUTH_SCOPES="read_user"
# Self-hosted GitLab only: the GitLab origin (default https://gitlab.com).
# The REST origin defaults to <base>/api/v4; override it only when your API
# lives elsewhere.
# GITLAB_BASE_URL=https://gitlab.example.com
# GITLAB_API_BASE_URL=https://gitlab.example.com/api/v4
# Optional hardening, same semantics as the GitHub allowlist above.
# GITLAB_OAUTH_ALLOWED_REDIRECT_URIS=https://updates.example.com/auth/callback

# Comma-separated allowlist of admin emails. Under invite-only registration,
# these emails may create their account on first OAuth sign-in (the rest need a
# pending invitation). Each must match the verified primary email of the admin's
# GitHub, Bitbucket, or GitLab account. Required under invite-only registration: the
# server refuses to start without it, since otherwise no one could ever sign in.
INITIAL_ADMIN_EMAILS=admin@example.com

# Account registration mode. Defaults to invite_only: an OAuth sign-in only
# creates an account if the email is in INITIAL_ADMIN_EMAILS or has a pending team
# invitation. Set to "open" to let any user with a verified GitHub, Bitbucket,
# or GitLab email self-register. Does
# not affect admin-driven `member provision` / `member invite`.
# REGISTRATION_MODE=invite_only
